<div class="definition-example lifecycle-definition" role="group" aria-labelledby="{{DEFINITION_PREFIX}}-title">
<div class="definition-preview">
<div class="definition-preview-top"><h3 id="{{DEFINITION_PREFIX}}-title">Lifecycle</h3></div>
<div class="definition-meaning"><div class="definition-hero"><figure class="definition-illustration"><img src="assets/definition-lifecycle-v1.png" alt="The same record in successive states, with preserved history and a closed gate before a possible future state." width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>A next state needs its own transition conditions.</figcaption></figure><div class="definition-core"><p>A lifecycle declares, for a kind of governed item, its states, initial state and permitted transitions. Each transition specifies the conditions that must be met and the effects it may produce. A particular item’s recorded state and transition history identify which changes actually occurred.</p>
</div></div>
<div class="definition-formal"><code>Lifecycle(C) = (States(C), Initial(C), T(C), Gates(C), Effects(C))</code><code>Permitted(x, C, s, t) ⇔ Applies(C, x) ∧ State_C(x) = s ∧ (s, t) ∈ T(C) ∧ GatesMet(C, x, s, t)</code></div></div>
<details class="lens-record-detail definition-record-detail definition-structure-detail"><summary>Read the full definition</summary>
<div class="definition-symbols"><p class="record-label">Symbols in the formula</p><dl class="definition-notation"><div><dt><var>x</var></dt><dd>The exact item version whose tracked state may change.</dd></div><div><dt><var>C</var></dt><dd>The governing lifecycle contract and its exact version.</dd></div><div><dt><code>States(C)</code>, <code>Initial(C)</code></dt><dd>The declared states and the initial state for items governed by the contract.</dd></div><div><dt><code>State_C(x)</code></dt><dd>The state of item <var>x</var> on the particular track governed by <var>C</var>. Record lifecycle, acceptance, response closure and view usability retain their own tracks.</dd></div><div><dt><var>s</var>, <var>t</var></dt><dd>The current state and proposed next state on that track.</dd></div><div><dt><code>Applies(C, x)</code></dt><dd>The contract governs this kind of item and this state track within the declared scope.</dd></div><div><dt><code>T(C)</code></dt><dd>The set of ordered state pairs whose transitions the contract permits.</dd></div><div><dt><code>Gates(C)</code>, <code>Effects(C)</code></dt><dd>The required conditions and declared effects for each transition.</dd></div><div><dt><code>GatesMet(C, x, s, t)</code></dt><dd>All conditions for this exact transition are met, including validation, review or an authorized owner’s decision where required.</dd></div></dl><p>This expression identifies a permitted change. Its execution and declared effects must also be recorded against the exact item and transition.</p></div>
<p class="record-label definition-scope-label">Scope and boundaries</p><dl class="definition-fields"><div><dt>Applicability</dt><dd>Items governed by a lifecycle contract: for example, a definition, implementation, project record or task view.</dd></div><div><dt>Exclusions</dt><dd>Establishing factual truth, granting general permission to act or advancing the state of another item.</dd></div><div><dt>Semantic owner and scope</dt><dd>The responsible owner maintains the contract for its declared item kind and scope. The authorized owner for a transition is identified by that contract.</dd></div><div><dt>Boundary conditions</dt><dd>Each item keeps its own lifecycle. A completed check, a recorded review and an acceptance decision have the distinct effects declared by the applicable contract.</dd></div></dl>
<div class="definition-voices"><dl><div><dt>Plain-language voice</dt><dd>The states something can be in, the conditions for moving between them and the record of what changed.</dd></div><div><dt>Domain-context voice</dt><dd>In the {{CASE_ACCOUNT}} incident, the agent recovers the completeness definition’s earlier acceptance, the current candidate fix and the incident’s open response. Closure, code activation and view expiry are assessed on their respective tracks.</dd></div></dl></div>
<div class="definition-review-fields"><dl class="definition-fields"><div><dt>Illustrative identity</dt><dd><code>example.definition.lifecycle</code> · version 1.0.0.</dd></div><div><dt>Source basis and standing</dt><dd>This explanatory definition is based on the Lab’s lifecycle contracts, including the Definition lifecycle profile. It is not a separately admitted Lifecycle definition.</dd></div><div><dt>Primary consumers</dt><dd>Item owners, reviewers, change operations and agents deciding which route a proposed change must follow.</dd></div><div><dt>Testable conditions</dt><dd>The item and contract are identifiable, the state pair is permitted, the transition conditions are recorded and the resulting effects stay within the contract.</dd></div><div><dt>Registry and index</dt><dd>No registry selection is created for this illustrative definition. A real definition would need exact owner acceptance and synchronized lookup records.</dd></div><div><dt>Drift and challenge route</dt><dd>Questions about state or transition evidence go to the responsible item owner. Changes to the governing contract follow that contract’s review and acceptance route.</dd></div></dl></div><p class="definition-image-credit">Illustration generated with imagegen. <a href="research/definition-card-images.json">Prompt and asset record</a>.</p></details>
</div>
</div>
